# reposync worktree snapshot: coordinator amendments (binding; override the mapped contract where they conflict)

Source: root review epic 7389668 comment 2026-09-26T19:46:34Z + user instruction: "Monorepo uses LFS, so blanket LFS-used => Complete=false would defeat the primary workload. Required changed/unpublished LFS content must survive, and size/budget omissions can never be Ready. This is an approved complete-WIP requirement, not added scope; keep tracked generated edits too."

## A1. No silent scope cuts; readiness is binary and honest
- `Snapshot.Complete` is true only when every piece of WIP is captured. There is no fixed per-file size omission (drop `MaxFileBytes`). Large files stream through the ArtifactSink like any other file (synckit chunks them).
- `MaxNewBytes` / `MaxEntries` become per-capture *progress budgets*, not omissions: when a capture hits a budget, the bytes already Put stay in the CAS and the ledger/stat cache records them, and Capture returns a `*PartialError{Remaining []string (paths), Reason}` (errors.As-able) instead of a Snapshot. The next capture resumes (stat-cache hits skip re-reads) until one completes. The caller (cc-sync) keeps the last complete checkpoint as the recovery point and reports the named remaining paths as the pause/incomplete reason. A partial capture never yields a Snapshot, so it can never be Ready.
- Remaining omission reasons are only those the package genuinely cannot capture: `submodule` (a submodule with its own local changes), `nested-repo` (an untracked nested git repo), `special-file` (fifo/socket/device). Any omission ⇒ `Complete=false`, and cc-sync must never mark such a checkpoint Ready (it retains the last complete one and surfaces the named paths). `changing` (a file that keeps changing across the retry) returns `ErrBusy` for the tick instead of an omission; the prior checkpoint is retained.
- No heuristic exclusions: `.gitignore`/info/exclude/core.excludesFile are the ONLY exclusion source. Tracked generated files with edits are WIP and are captured like any other tracked file.

## A2. Git LFS is first-class (primary monorepo uses LFS)
Never shell out to anything that writes the source (git-lfs clean/smudge/fetch/checkout write `.git/lfs` or the index). Pure reads only:
- Detect LFS paths with `git check-attr -z --stdin filter` (read-only) on candidate paths; `filter=lfs` marks an LFS path.
- Worktree changes (Y column) on LFS paths: status runs with clean/process filters overridden to empty, so a stat-dirty but content-identical LFS file appears modified. For each such path compute the canonical LFS pointer of the raw bytes yourself (`version https://git-lfs.github.com/spec/v1\noid sha256:<hex>\nsize <n>\n`; honor any extra pointer keys only if present in the index pointer) and compare with the index blob: identical ⇒ not WIP (drop); different ⇒ a real edit: ship the RAW working bytes as a MediaFile (never ship a pointer as the content of an unpublished edit).
- Staged LFS content (X column where the index blob is an LFS pointer): ship the pointer blob (MediaBlob) AND the referenced LFS object from `<CommonDir>/lfs/objects/<oid[0:2]>/<oid[2:4]>/<oid>` as a new media `MediaLFSObject = "application/vnd.git-lfs.object"` (content-addressed; verify sha256 == pointer oid and size while streaming).
- Unpublished history (commits in `HEAD ^TrunkTip`): enumerate LFS pointers introduced by those commits with pure git reads (e.g. `git log --format= --raw --no-abbrev -z HEAD ^<TrunkTip>` / `git diff-tree`, then `cat-file --batch` on small blobs that parse as pointers, only for paths with filter=lfs), and ship each referenced object from the local LFS store as MediaLFSObject. CAS dedup reuses unchanged objects across ticks.
- A required LFS object (staged or unpublished-history) that is absent from the local LFS store is NOT silently dropped: Capture returns `*MissingLFSError{Objects []LFSObjectRef{Path, OID, Size}}` (errors.As-able); cc-sync retains the last complete checkpoint and names the objects.
- Published base assets (LFS objects referenced by trunk-reachable content) are NOT shipped: they are fetched on the receiver like trunk commits. Record `Snapshot.LFS *LFSInfo{Objects []LFSObjectRef (shipped: path, oid, size, artifact ref), Remote string}`.
- Snapshot manifest: add `LFSObjects []LFSObject{OID string; Size int64; Artifact ArtifactRef}` to the manifest; `Artifacts()` includes them.
- Verify: every shipped LFS object must be present in the ArtifactSource (Has) and hash-verified on import; Ready requires all of them.
- Restore: with `GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=1` for the worktree add/read-trees, then write every shipped object into the receiver's `<CommonDir>/lfs/objects/...` (temp + rename, sha256 re-verified), then hydrate LFS pointer files in the recovery worktree from the local LFS store with `git -C <Dest> lfs checkout -- <paths>` restricted to paths whose objects are local (this writes only the new recovery worktree, which is allowed). Base assets not local are fetched with `git -C <Dest> lfs fetch` + `lfs checkout` only when `RestoreOptions.FetchLFS` is true (cc-sync sets it only when network policy allows bulk); otherwise `Restored.LFSPending` lists the still-pointer paths and fidelity reports them. Raw-bytes MediaFile entries are applied after hydration and always win.
- Tests (required): an LFS repo fixture using real git-lfs 3.x with a local file-based LFS remote (`lfs.url` / `lfs.standalonetransferagent` or a `file://` remote) covering: (1) capture of a repo using LFS with no LFS changes is Complete (not blanket-incomplete); (2) modified LFS-tracked worktree binary ships raw bytes and restores byte-identical; (3) staged new LFS file ships pointer + object and restores staged pointer in index and real bytes on disk; (4) an unpushed commit adding an LFS file ships the object and the restored recovery branch hydrates it with no network; (5) a stat-touched but unchanged LFS file is not captured; (6) a required object deleted from the source LFS store yields MissingLFSError naming it; (7) the source `.git/lfs` directory is byte-identical before/after capture (no clean filter ran).

## A3. Capture budgets vs. the scheduler
Default budgets: `MaxNewBytes = 1 GiB` per capture tick, `MaxEntries = 200_000` per capture. Both are progress budgets per A1, never omissions.

# Mapped contract
## CONTRACT
Package github.com/yasyf/reposync/worktree (new, exported). It imports registry and internal/vcs and has no synckit dependency.

// ---- artifacts (adapted by cc-sync onto synckit's CAS) ----
type Media string
const (
  MediaBundle   Media = "application/x-git-bundle"                        // already zlib/delta-compressed; synckit should skip zstd
  MediaBlob     Media = "application/vnd.git.blob"                        // staged blob bytes, no filters
  MediaFile     Media = "application/octet-stream"                        // raw worktree bytes or symlink target
  MediaSnapshot Media = "application/vnd.reposync.worktree-snapshot+json" // Encode(Snapshot)
)
type ArtifactRef struct {
  Digest string `json:"digest"` // "sha256:<64 hex>"
  Size   int64  `json:"size"`
  Media  Media  `json:"media"`
}
// ArtifactSink stores captured content. Put hashes while writing, is durable before returning, and is idempotent for an existing digest.
type ArtifactSink interface {
  Has(ctx context.Context, refs []ArtifactRef) ([]bool, error)
  Put(ctx context.Context, media Media, r io.Reader) (ArtifactRef, error)
}
// ArtifactSource reads verified content. Open yields exactly ref.Size bytes whose sha256 matches ref.Digest, or an error.
type ArtifactSource interface {
  Has(ctx context.Context, refs []ArtifactRef) ([]bool, error)
  Open(ctx context.Context, ref ArtifactRef) (io.ReadCloser, error)
}

// ---- discovery ----
type Kind string
const (
  KindGit         Kind = "git"          // main or linked git worktree
  KindJJColocated Kind = "jj-colocated" // default workspace of a colocated jj repo; git HEAD is @-
  KindJJWorkspace Kind = "jj-workspace" // secondary jj workspace: no .git, read through the repo's git store
)
type Worktree struct {
  ID          string `json:"id"`          // hex(sha256(origin \x00 root \x00 incarnation))[:32]
  Origin      string `json:"origin"`      // registry key; how peers find their checkout
  Relpath     string `json:"relpath"`
  Trunk       string `json:"trunk"`
  Root        string `json:"root"`        // symlink-resolved worktree root
  GitDir      string `json:"git_dir"`     // per-worktree admin dir (== CommonDir for main); "" for jj-workspace
  CommonDir   string `json:"common_dir"`
  Name        string `json:"name"`        // "" main; <common>/worktrees/<name> basename; or jj workspace name
  Kind        Kind   `json:"kind"`
  Branch      string `json:"branch,omitempty"` // short name; "" when detached
  Head        string `json:"head"`
  Incarnation uint64 `json:"incarnation"` // inode of the per-worktree admin dir (jj: <ws>/.jj/working_copy); a worktree recreated at the same path gets a new ID
  Locked      bool   `json:"locked,omitempty"`
}
type Skip struct{ Path, Reason string }
func Discover(ctx context.Context, reg registry.Registry) ([]Worktree, []Skip, error)
func Locate(wts []Worktree, path string) (Worktree, bool) // longest symlink-resolved Root prefix containing path (session cwd → worktree)
Discover algorithm:
- For each reg.Repos entry with !LocalOnly && Origin != "": a missing checkout becomes a Skip.
- Common dir: if root/.git is a dir, use it; if it is a file, parse `gitdir:` and then <gitdir>/commondir.
- `git -C root worktree list --porcelain -z`: skip bare and prunable entries. Linked GitDir comes from parsing <wt>/.git and must lie under <common>/worktrees/.
- If root/.jj is a dir: the main entry becomes KindJJColocated, and `jj --ignore-working-copy --at-op=@ -R root workspace list -T 'name ++ "\t" ++ root ++ "\n"'` adds the non-default workspaces as KindJJWorkspace.

// ---- snapshot manifest (stored as a MediaSnapshot artifact; only Summary goes in catalog metadata) ----
const SnapshotSchema = "reposync-worktree-snapshot-v1"
type Snapshot struct {
  Schema       string       `json:"schema"`
  Digest       string       `json:"digest"`   // sha256 of Encode() with Digest="" and CapturedAt zero; equal digest == unchanged WIP
  Source       string       `json:"source"`   // synckit host id (CaptureOptions.Source)
  Worktree     Worktree     `json:"worktree"`
  CapturedAt   time.Time    `json:"captured_at"`
  ObjectFormat string       `json:"object_format"` // "sha1" | "sha256"
  Head         Head         `json:"head"`
  History      []Bundle     `json:"history"`   // chain, oldest first; importing in order yields Head.Commit; empty when Head is on origin/<trunk>
  Requires     []string     `json:"requires"`  // commits the receiver must already hold (published trunk): chain[0] prerequisites ∪ later-link prerequisites not produced by earlier links; [Head.Commit] when History is empty
  Index        []IndexEntry `json:"index"`     // HEAD→index delta, sorted by path
  Files        []FileEntry  `json:"files"`     // index→worktree delta incl. untracked, sorted by path
  IntentToAdd  []string     `json:"intent_to_add,omitempty"`
  Sparse       *Sparse      `json:"sparse,omitempty"`
  JJ           *JJ          `json:"jj,omitempty"`
  Omitted      []Omission   `json:"omitted,omitempty"`
  Complete     bool         `json:"complete"`  // len(Omitted)==0
}
type Head struct {
  Commit    string `json:"commit"`
  Branch    string `json:"branch,omitempty"`
  Upstream  string `json:"upstream,omitempty"`
  TrunkTip  string `json:"trunk_tip"`  // origin/<trunk> on the source at capture
  TrunkBase string `json:"trunk_base"` // merge-base(HEAD, TrunkTip)
  Ahead     int    `json:"ahead"`      // rev-list --count HEAD ^TrunkTip
}
type Bundle struct { Artifact ArtifactRef `json:"artifact"`; Tip string `json:"tip"`; Prerequisites []string `json:"prerequisites"` }
type IndexEntry struct {
  Path string       `json:"path"`
  Mode string       `json:"mode"`           // "100644"|"100755"|"120000"|"160000"; "" = removed from the index
  OID  string       `json:"oid,omitempty"`
  Blob *ArtifactRef `json:"blob,omitempty"` // nil for removals and gitlinks
}
type FileKind string // "file" | "symlink" | "deleted"
type FileEntry struct {
  Path       string       `json:"path"`
  Kind       FileKind     `json:"kind"`
  Executable bool         `json:"executable,omitempty"`
  Content    *ArtifactRef `json:"content,omitempty"` // raw bytes / symlink target; nil when deleted
  Untracked  bool         `json:"untracked,omitempty"`
}
type Sparse struct { Cone bool `json:"cone"`; Patterns []string `json:"patterns"` }
type JJ struct { Workspace, ChangeID, WorkingCopyCommit, Description string; Bookmarks []string } // json snake_case; informational (as of jj's last snapshot)
type OmissionReason string // "too-large" | "budget" | "changing" | "submodule" | "nested-repo" | "special-file" | "lfs-objects" | "history-too-large"
type Omission struct { Path string `json:"path"`; Reason OmissionReason `json:"reason"`; Size int64 `json:"size,omitempty"` }
type Summary struct { WorktreeID, Branch, Head, Digest string; CapturedAt time.Time; Ahead, Staged, Unstaged, Untracked, Omitted int; Bytes int64; Complete bool }
func (s Snapshot) Artifacts() []ArtifactRef // deduped closure: bundles + blobs + files; bound ≤ 2*Limits.MaxEntries + MaxChainLinks
func (s Snapshot) Summary() Summary
func Encode(Snapshot) ([]byte, error) // canonical JSON
func Decode([]byte) (Snapshot, error) // validates schema, sort order, digest; paths must be clean, relative, NUL-free, with no ".." and no ".git" component (case-insensitive)

// ---- store + operations ----
type Store struct{ /* root */ }
func OpenStore(root string) (*Store, error)
// Layout under root:
//   locks/<repokey>.lock
//   source/<repokey>.git        (bare scratch; objects/info/alternates → source common objects; gc.auto=0)
//   source/<wtid>.json          (ledger: stat cache path→{dev,ino,size,mtime_ns,ctime_ns,ref}, oid→ref, history chain [{tip,excludes,ref,bytes}])
//   source/<wtid>.index         (jj-workspace private index)
//   mirror/<repokey>.git        (receiver: bare, alternates → receiver checkout common objects, gc.auto=0)
//   mirror/<repokey>.json       (receiver refcounts: pins oid→{snapshot keys}, tips)
// repokey = hex(sha256(origin))[:16].
type Limits struct { MaxFileBytes int64 /*100 MiB*/; MaxNewBytes int64 /*1 GiB read per capture*/; MaxEntries int /*50_000*/; MaxHistoryBytes int64 /*512 MiB*/; MaxChainLinks int /*8*/ }
type CaptureOptions struct { Source string; Limits Limits }
var ErrBusy = errors.New("worktree busy")          // live lock, or HEAD/index moved mid-capture: retry, keep prior checkpoint
type DeferredError struct{ Reason string }          // merge/rebase/cherry-pick/revert/bisect/am/sequencer, unmerged index, jj conflict or merge @
var ErrRepoAbsent = errors.New("repo not checked out on this host")
var ErrDestinationExists = errors.New("recovery destination exists")
var ErrPathCollision = errors.New("snapshot paths collide on this filesystem")
func (s *Store) Capture(ctx context.Context, wt Worktree, sink ArtifactSink, opts CaptureOptions) (Snapshot, error)
type VerifyOptions struct{ FetchOrigin bool } // allow `git fetch origin <trunk>` when Requires are missing; cc-sync gates this on network policy
type Verification struct { Ready bool; Missing []string; Checkout string }
func (s *Store) Verify(ctx context.Context, reg registry.Registry, snap Snapshot, src ArtifactSource, opts VerifyOptions) (Verification, error)
type RestoreOptions struct { Dest string /*required, absolute, must not exist*/; Branch string /*"" → recovery/<branch|detached>-<source>-<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>*/; Fresh bool /*ignore an existing sibling checkout, still never overwrite*/ }
type Restored struct { Path, Branch, Head string; Reused bool; Applied string /*digest applied in Path*/; Newer bool /*snap is newer than Applied*/; LFSPending bool; Exact bool; Differences []string }
func (s *Store) Restore(ctx context.Context, reg registry.Registry, snap Snapshot, src ArtifactSource, opts RestoreOptions) (Restored, error)
func (s *Store) Release(ctx context.Context, reg registry.Registry, snap Snapshot) error // receiver: drop mirror refs and unpin at refcount zero; source: prune chain ledger entries no retained snapshot names
+ registry: func (r Registry) ByOrigin(origin string) (Repo, bool)

CAPTURE (all source calls: GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0, gitConfigEnv, filter.<d>.clean/process="" and required=false for every configured driver):
0. Take the per-repo flock. Stat the marker files with no subprocess. <GitDir>/{MERGE_HEAD,rebase-merge,rebase-apply,CHERRY_PICK_HEAD,REVERT_HEAD,BISECT_LOG,sequencer} return DeferredError. <GitDir>/index.lock, <CommonDir>/packed-refs.lock, and root/.jj/working_copy/working_copy.lock or .jj/repo/git_import_export.lock return ErrBusy. Record the stat of <GitDir>/index and the content of <GitDir>/HEAD.
1. jj kinds: run `jj --ignore-working-copy --at-op=@ -R <root> log --no-graph -r 'conflicts() & ::<ws>@ & mutable()' -T 'commit_id ++ "\n"'`. Non-empty output returns DeferredError("jj conflict"). Also read `<ws>@` change_id/commit_id/description and `<ws>@-` local_bookmarks. For jj-workspace only: a multi-parent @- returns Deferred. Otherwise `GIT_DIR=<common> GIT_INDEX_FILE=source/<wtid>.index git read-tree -m <ws@->` then `git update-index -q --refresh`; this is the private index, so writing it is allowed.
2. One consistent read: `git -C <root> status --porcelain=v2 -z --branch --untracked-files=all --ignored=no --no-renames --ignore-submodules=none` (jj-workspace adds GIT_DIR/GIT_WORK_TREE/GIT_INDEX_FILE and `-- . ':(exclude,top).jj'`). Parse:
   - `# branch.oid` gives Head.Commit; `# branch.head` gives Branch; `# branch.upstream` gives Upstream.
   - `1 XY sub mH mI mW hH hI path`:
     - X!='.' gives IndexEntry{Mode:mI (000000→""), OID:hI}. Ignored for jj-workspace.
     - Y=='D' gives FileEntry deleted. Y in M/T gives FileEntry from lstat. Y=='A' gives FileEntry plus IntentToAdd.
     - sub!="N..." with any change gives Omission submodule.
   - `u ...` returns DeferredError("unmerged paths").
   - `? path`: a trailing '/' (nested repo) gives Omission nested-repo; otherwise FileEntry{Untracked:true}.
3. Resolve TrunkTip = rev-parse refs/remotes/origin/<trunk>; TrunkBase = merge-base; Ahead = rev-list --count HEAD ^TrunkTip.
4. History (Ahead>0). Load the ledger chain.
   - If tail.Tip==HEAD and `cat-file -e` succeeds, reuse the chain.
   - Else if len<MaxChainLinks and `merge-base --is-ancestor tail.Tip HEAD`, append a link: `git --git-dir=source/<repokey>.git update-ref refs/capture/<wtid>/tip <HEAD>`, then `git --git-dir=<scratch> bundle create <tmp> refs/capture/<wtid>/tip ^<tail.Tip> ^<TrunkTip>`.
   - Else start a new chain with `bundle create <tmp> refs/capture/<wtid>/tip ^<TrunkTip>`.
   Parse the '-<oid>' prerequisite header lines, check `bundle list-heads` tip==HEAD, then Put(MediaBundle). Over MaxHistoryBytes gives Omission history-too-large.
5. Staged blobs (modes 100644/100755/120000): the ledger maps oid→ref; if Has, skip. Otherwise stream through one `git cat-file --batch` into Put(MediaBlob).
6. Files: lstat under os.OpenRoot(root).
   - Symlink: Readlink.
   - Regular file: if the stat-cache hit and Has, reuse. Otherwise stream into Put(MediaFile), then re-lstat; if it changed, retry once, then Omission changing.
   - Budgets: size>MaxFileBytes gives Omission too-large, exceeding MaxNewBytes gives budget, and fifo/socket/device gives special-file.
   - Executable = mode&0o111 (or mI when core.fileMode=false).
   - LFS: if the repo uses LFS and Ahead>0, Omission lfs-objects, conservatively.
7. Re-check the markers, <GitDir>/HEAD, and the index stat. Any change returns ErrBusy. Read sparse patterns from <GitDir>/info/sparse-checkout when core.sparseCheckout is set. Sort, compute Complete and Digest, persist the ledger with durable.WriteFile, and return. An unchanged worktree yields the same Digest with zero Put calls.

VERIFY (receiver, per-repo lock):
1. Look up the checkout with reg.ByOrigin; if absent, return ErrRepoAbsent. The object format must match.
2. Ensure the mirror: `git init --bare --object-format=<fmt>`, alternates → <checkout common>/objects, gc.auto=0.
3. Pin Requires in the checkout with `git -C <checkout> update-ref --stdin` (`update refs/reposync/pins/<oid> <oid>`). Update-ref refuses a nonexistent object, so the pin doubles as the presence check. On failure with FetchOrigin, run `git -C <checkout> fetch --no-tags --no-write-fetch-head origin +refs/heads/<trunk>:refs/remotes/origin/<trunk>` and retry. Still missing returns Ready=false with Missing.
4. For each link lacking mirror ref refs/snapshots/tips/<tip>: spool the artifact to a private temp file, run `git --git-dir=<mirror> bundle verify`, then `git --git-dir=<mirror> fetch --no-tags --no-write-fetch-head <file> refs/capture/<wtid>/tip:refs/snapshots/tips/<tip>`. Fetch runs index-pack and the connectivity check.
5. Hash each IndexEntry blob with `git --git-dir=<mirror> hash-object -w --no-filters --stdin[-paths]`; the OID must equal entry.OID. Then build the staged tree: `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp> read-tree <Head.Commit>`, `update-index --index-info` (lines `<mode> <oid>\t<path>`, or `0 <zero-oid>\t<path>` for a removal), `write-tree`, `commit-tree -p <Head.Commit>`, and set refs/snapshots/<wtid>/<digest>/{head,index}.
6. src.Has(Files contents) must all be true. Record refcounts and return Ready=true.

RESTORE (receiver, per-repo lock):
1. Run Verify; it must return Ready.
2. Unless Fresh, scan `git -C <checkout> worktree list --porcelain -z` for <admin>/reposync/recovery.json matching {source, worktree_id, incarnation}. A match returns Restored{Reused:true, Applied, Newer} and touches no files.
3. Lstat(Dest) must be ENOENT, else ErrDestinationExists. `refs/heads/<branch>` must not exist; if it does, suffix -2, -3, and so on.
4. On a case-insensitive destination volume, case-fold or NFC-duplicate paths in Files∪IntentToAdd return ErrPathCollision.
5. Run with `-c core.hooksPath=/dev/null` and GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=1:
   - `git -C <checkout> fetch --no-tags --no-write-fetch-head <mirror> +refs/snapshots/<wtid>/<digest>/*:refs/reposync/recovery/<d12>/*`
   - `git -C <checkout> worktree add --no-checkout -b <branch> <Dest> refs/reposync/recovery/<d12>/head`
   - `git -C <Dest> read-tree -u --reset HEAD`
   - `git -C <Dest> read-tree -m -u HEAD refs/reposync/recovery/<d12>/index^{tree}`. The index is now the source index and staged files are on disk.
6. Apply Files through os.OpenRoot(Dest): deleted → Remove; symlink → Remove then Symlink; file → temp in the same dir, Rename, then chmod 0644 or 0755.
7. Run `git -C <Dest> add -N --pathspec-from-file=- --pathspec-file-nul` for IntentToAdd with GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1.
8. Delete refs/reposync/recovery/<d12>/*. Write <Dest admin>/reposync/recovery.json via durable.WriteFile.
9. Recompute porcelain v2 and compare it with the manifest-derived expectation; the result sets Exact and Differences.


## STEPS
- internal/vcs/exec.go: add an exported streaming runner, e.g. `type Cmd struct{Dir, Name string; Args, Env []string; Stdin io.Reader; Stdout io.Writer}` + `func Exec(ctx context.Context, c Cmd) error` (same Setpgid/SIGTERM/opTimeout/cmdError semantics). Re-express run/runStdin on it. Export `ReadOnlyGitEnv()` = gitConfigEnv + GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 + GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0.
- internal/vcs/inprogress.go: split markers into per-worktree (index.lock, MERGE_HEAD, rebase-merge, rebase-apply, CHERRY_PICK_HEAD, REVERT_HEAD, BISECT_LOG, + sequencer) and common (packed-refs.lock). Add `OpState(gitDir, commonDir, jjDir string) (reason string, lock bool, err error)` and keep OpInProgress(root) as the root/.git wrapper so current callers are unchanged. Unit-test ENOTDIR avoidance on a linked worktree.
- registry/registry.go: add `func (r Registry) ByOrigin(origin string) (Repo, bool)` (propagating entries only).
- worktree/artifact.go + snapshot.go: types, Media consts, Encode/Decode/validate (path safety, sort, schema), Digest, Artifacts, Summary.
- worktree/discover.go + jj.go: common-dir resolution from .git dir/pointer file, `git worktree list --porcelain -z` parser, jj `workspace list` read with --ignore-working-copy --at-op=@, inode incarnation, ID, Locate.
- worktree/status.go: porcelain v2 -z --branch parser (1/u/?/# records, sub field, mode/oid columns) plus the filter-override env builder from `git config -z --get-regexp '^filter\..*\.(clean|process)$'`.
- worktree/store.go: OpenStore, layout, per-repo durable.AcquireLock, ledgers (durable.WriteFile), scratch bare repo init with alternates, mirror init with alternates and object-format.
- worktree/history.go: chain decision (reuse/append/new), scratch update-ref, bundle create/list-heads/prerequisite header parse, Requires computation.
- worktree/capture.go: marker gate, jj probes, jj-workspace private index refresh, status read, cat-file --batch blob streaming, os.Root-based file reads with stat cache and re-stat race check, limits and omissions, final HEAD/index recheck.
- worktree/verify.go: pin-by-update-ref transaction, optional origin trunk fetch, bundle import into mirror, staged-tree rebuild with OID checks, file artifact presence, refcounts. Release in the same file.
- worktree/restore.go: sibling marker scan, destination/branch/case-collision checks, hook-less LFS-skip worktree add + two read-trees, os.Root file layer, i-t-a, marker write, fidelity status diff.
- internal/vcs/vcstest/vcstest.go: add `LinkedWorktree(repo, dest, branch)`, `JJWorkspace(repo, dest, name)`, `SnapshotTree(dir)` (bytes+modes+symlinks map) helpers.
- Tests beside each file (see test_plan). Then CHANGELOG [Unreleased] Added entry, README library section (writing-docs + slop-cop), and a v0.31.0 release that cc-sync pins.

## TESTS
- Discover (table-driven, real git/jj via vcstest): main + two linked worktrees (one locked, one prunable → skipped), a local-only registered repo skipped, a colocated jj repo with a secondary workspace (KindJJWorkspace, root from template), a registered path whose .git is a pointer file. IDs are stable across calls and change after `worktree remove` + re-add at the same path (incarnation). Locate picks the longest root.
- Read-only proof: sha256 every file under CommonDir, GitDir, and .jj before/after Capture on git, jj-colocated, and jj-workspace sources; assert identical bytes and no new files (the experiment showed this with the index and common dir). Include an LFS-configured repo with a fake clean filter that writes a sentinel file; the sentinel must stay absent.
- Round-trip fidelity to a separate clone: partial staging (text and binary with distinct staged and worktree versions), staged delete, unstaged delete, exec-bit change, symlink (tracked and untracked), i-t-a, untracked files in nested dirs, Unicode/punctuation/newline/tab filenames, detached HEAD, two unpushed commits across two capture ticks (chain of 2). Ignored node_modules/ and *.log are absent from the Snapshot and the restore. Assert porcelain v2 set equality, byte and mode equality of the worktree, `git log` SHA equality, and Restored.Exact.
- Deferral: MERGE_HEAD, rebase-merge, rebase-apply (am), CHERRY_PICK_HEAD, REVERT_HEAD, BISECT_LOG, sequencer, unmerged index, and a jj conflicted @ each return *DeferredError. index.lock and jj working_copy.lock return ErrBusy. A HEAD moved by a counting sink hook mid-capture returns ErrBusy.
- Incremental/zero payload: a second capture with no change gives an equal Digest and zero Put calls. One edited file gives exactly one MediaFile Put. A new commit appends one chain link. An amend starts a new chain. A chain reaching MaxChainLinks starts a new chain. The stat cache avoids re-reading an unchanged dirty 50 MB untracked file.
- Limits/completeness: a file over MaxFileBytes gives Omission too-large with Complete=false. Exceeding MaxEntries or MaxNewBytes gives budget omissions. A FIFO gives special-file. A dirty submodule gives submodule. A nested repo gives nested-repo. A shallow source with a boundary past the trunk base gives an error or history omission, never a silent partial.
- Verify: receiver clone behind the source trunk with FetchOrigin=false gives Ready=false and Missing=[trunk base]; with FetchOrigin=true against a local bare origin it gives Ready. A source returning corrupt bundle bytes, a truncated bundle, or a blob whose git OID mismatches gives an error and no mirror refs. A missing file artifact gives Ready=false. Pins exist after Verify and disappear after Release of the last referencing snapshot. Re-Verify is idempotent.
- Relay: verify on host B, then copy only B's artifacts to host C (source repo deleted) and Verify+Restore on C gives Exact.
- Restore safety: an existing Dest gives ErrDestinationExists with untouched contents. An existing branch name gets a -2 suffix. A post-checkout hook writing a sentinel does not run. Sibling pickup: restore, edit a file in the checkout, then restore a newer snapshot for the same worktree; this gives Reused=true and Newer=true with the edit intact (no rollback, no reapply). Fresh=true creates a second checkout.
- Case/Unicode: Files containing A.txt+a.txt (or NFC/NFD twins) restored onto a case-insensitive APFS temp volume give ErrPathCollision. Skip on case-sensitive filesystems.
- jj-colocated source restored into a plain-git receiver: commits between trunk and @- land on the recovery branch and the unsnapshotted edits appear as unstaged. The Snapshot.JJ description is carried.
- sha256 object-format repo round trip (bundle v3).
- go vet ./..., go test -race ./..., golangci-lint via task lint.

## OPEN
- Stash entries (refs/stash, shared across worktrees): capture them? Default: no. The stash is common-dir-wide and not per-session WIP. Record `stash_count` in Summary so the UI can warn.
- Other unpushed local branches that are not in HEAD's ancestry: capture? Default: no. Only HEAD ancestry per worktree, per the contract's worktree scope.
- Sparse-checkout sources: re-apply the sparse patterns on restore? That flips extensions.worktreeConfig on the receiver repo, and jj compatibility with it is unverified. Default: restore a full checkout and return Snapshot.Sparse for the user or cc-sync to re-apply.
- Receiver pins as refs/reposync/pins/<oid> inside the user's registered checkout (visible to `git for-each-ref`/`log --all`, ignored by jj import): acceptable? Default: yes. There is one ref per distinct trunk prerequisite, refcounted; everything else lives in the private mirror.
- LFS objects referenced by unpushed commits or staged pointers: ship <common>/lfs/objects as artifacts now, or mark them incomplete? Default for v1: detect LFS usage and add Omission lfs-objects (Complete=false). Restore uses GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 and reports LFSPending.
- Should Restore auto-apply a newer snapshot to a reused sibling checkout that is still pristine (status matches its recorded restored state)? Default: no. Report Newer and let the user choose Fresh, per 'never rollback / never reapply stale code'.
- Verify's origin fetch: cc-sync passes FetchOrigin only when its network policy allows bulk. Default: cc-sync owns the decision and the package never fetches on its own.
- Should reposync ship a debug CLI (`reposync worktree list --json`)? Default: no. cc-sync owns the user-facing CLI, keeping reposync's change minimal.

## RISKS
- Source filters: overriding clean/process filters keeps git-lfs from writing .git/lfs/objects. The cost is that stat-dirty LFS or filtered files show as modified and ship raw bytes. The result is still correct but uses more bandwidth. Custom filters with required=true need the required=false override too.
- GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 means the source index never gets refreshed stat info from our reads. In a huge monorepo without fsmonitor, each status re-lstats and re-hashes racy entries. The scheduler's tiers (2m/5m/15m/1h) and the two-worker cap bound the cost, but a 100k-file status may still take seconds.
- Source gc can prune objects that only scratch refs reference (after an amend or reset). The ledger must `cat-file -e` chain tips before reuse and start a new chain on failure. The durable copy is always the bundle artifact.
- The receiver mirror depends on the checkout's ODB through alternates. A re-clone or deletion of the checkout breaks mirror refs. Verify must detect missing mirror objects (`cat-file -e` on refs) and rebuild from artifacts. Pins protect trunk prerequisites from gc but not from a force-pushed trunk.
- A trunk commit that is later force-pushed away upstream becomes an unsatisfiable Requires on receivers that never fetched it. Verify reports Missing (no false ACK), and the source must re-capture against the new trunk.
- jj reads with --ignore-working-copy --at-op=@ reflect jj's last snapshot. The @ description and conflict flag can be stale relative to unsnapshotted edits. The git view (captured files) is authoritative. The jj template keywords (`root`, `conflicts()`, `mutable()`) are version-sensitive; verified only on 0.45.1.
- A torn read of a file an agent is writing is mitigated by re-lstat, retry, and Omission changing. A HEAD or index moved mid-capture returns ErrBusy, and the prior checkpoint is retained.
- Restoring a full monorepo checkout (read-tree -u of HEAD) can take minutes and a lot of disk. With LFS skip-smudge, pointer files stay unhydrated until `git lfs pull`.
- Many small untracked files produce many artifacts (up to MaxEntries). synckit batching must coalesce them within the 32 MiB batch cap, or per-artifact overhead will dominate.
- Path identity: macOS is case-insensitive and normalizes Unicode. A session cwd typed with different case may not match Worktree.Root under Locate. Canonicalize via realpath and fall back to case-fold comparison only on case-insensitive volumes.
